plan Inspection
title   Inspection: structural representation, live inspection, evidence, traces and redaction
status  proposed
goal
    An agent or a host can learn how an application is built and what it did without reading much
    code and without gaining execution authority. flow-state/inspect offers inspect, render,
    attachInspector and trace. Observation never starts work, never changes committed state,
    never persists implicitly, never invents evidence and never delays a turn. CLI leaves and
    flags are @plan.FlowCommandLine.O1 to @plan.FlowCommandLine.O5. Full notes:
    references/full/Inspection.full.plan.txt.
outcomes
    O1  inspect(app) and inspect(runtime)
        inspect(app) returns a Promise of a plain readonly Model. It loads every lazy entry
        (@plan.LazyFeatures.O5) and compiles the App without running any machine callback,
        guard, adapter, Layer or service; it builds no Runtime and touches no storage. A failed
        load rejects with EntryLoadFailed naming the entry; a construction error rejects with
        the construction FlowError. Equal App code gives equal Model bytes on every host.
        The Model lists the app and entries (eager or lazy), each machine's schemas, states,
        transitions, guards with their possible deny codes, actions, schedules, activities,
        outcome edges, context reads and persistence opt-ins, every operation descriptor, each
        entity domain as recorded, the static actor-send graph and a fingerprint. It holds no
        live state, closures, Effects or services. A constant in a condition is a literal up
        to 256 bytes, otherwise its hash; a sensitive constant is never exported raw.

        inspect(runtime) is synchronous and passive. It reads the last committed cut and never
        a turn in progress, starts no work, adopts nothing, touches no storage and emits no
        evidence. It works on a constructed, ready, disposing or disposed Runtime. It returns
        frozen plain data with faithful in-process values: runtime lifecycle, clock and
        evidence sequence; per actor its state, revision, mailbox depth, schedules, activity
        bindings, pending commits, issues and retain; resource entries, transactions and stream
        sessions in flight; per domain the answers with frontier and stale causes, predictions,
        queues and uncertain records; persistence status, including SecondTabNoPersistence;
        lazy entries adopted or not; hydration provenance. Revisions, timers and generations
        appear here and in evidence, never in public snapshots.

        For flow explain on a checkpoint, one snapshot and one event give the handler levels
        tried with guard results, the target or "self", ordered exits and entries, the memory
        patch, and the schedules and activities the new configuration would start. It runs
        only pure callbacks, labels every field desired, never claims a running fiber, and
        agrees with check on Allowed, Denied and Unhandled (@plan.Machines.O4).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/tools/inspection.ts.
    O2  The structural codec
        One bounded codec carries every value that crosses a process or storage boundary:
        persisted records (@plan.FlowPersistence.O3), server-rendering payloads
        (@plan.ServerRendering.O3), trace artifacts and the Model bytes. It accepts JSON values
        produced by the owning Schema. A value at a bound is accepted, one past it is
        rejected: depth 32, 10,000 nodes, 4,096 items per array, 262,144 UTF-8 bytes per
        string or key, 2,097,152 canonical bytes.

        Encoding is canonical: sorted keys, no whitespace, no -0, NaN, infinity, cycles, lone
        surrogates or sparse arrays. Decoding is strict: it rejects a byte-order mark, invalid
        UTF-8, and any input that does not re-encode to exactly the same bytes (duplicate
        keys, unsorted keys, odd number spellings), then decodes with the owning Schema.
        Equal accepted data gives equal bytes on every host; mutating an input or output byte
        array afterwards never changes any stored or decoded value.

        A failure is one FlowError raised before any actor, store, service or committed change:
        CodecBoundExceeded (naming the bound, its value and the path), CodecMalformed (with a
        reason such as utf8, json, nonCanonical, cycle, negativeZero, nonFinite, loneSurrogate,
        notPlain or sparse) or CodecSchemaMismatch (path and issue summary, never data values).
        No raw parser error or Cause escapes; what to do about it belongs to the caller.
    O3  Inspector sinks
        attachInspector(runtime, callback) attaches a callback; attachInspector(runtime,
        { capacity? }) creates a bounded buffer. Both return drain() and dispose(); the buffer
        also returns snapshot() and clear(). Capacity defaults to 256 and may be zero; an
        invalid capacity throws InvalidInspectorCapacity before attaching. snapshot() returns
        { records, truncatedBeforeSequence }, where the marker is null only if nothing was
        omitted, otherwise the greatest omitted or cleared sequence. Overflow drops the oldest
        records and advances the marker. clear() empties the window without resetting the
        sequence.

        A sink belongs to one Runtime and attaches once (InspectorAlreadyAttached on reuse);
        attaching to a disposed Runtime rejects with RuntimeDisposed. Attaching before ready()
        sees bootstrap evidence. A late attachment first gets a redacted current snapshot and
        a marker for what it missed, then later records only.

        Sinks never delay or change anything. Records are accepted with publication, delivered
        asynchronously in sequence order, and never run user code inside a turn. A slow sink
        never blocks turns, sends, context waves or persistence. A callback that throws detaches
        only itself; drain and dispose reject with InspectorSinkFailed and a diagnostic reaches
        healthy sinks. drain() waits for the evidence accepted when it was called; dispose()
        delivers that prefix, detaches, is idempotent, and no callback runs after it resolves.
        Runtime disposal accepts terminal lifecycle evidence for consumers before providers.
        No omission is silent and no buffer is persisted.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/tools/inspection.ts.
    O4  Traces
        Every Runtime decision is an evidence event with one Runtime-global sequence starting
        at 1, so events of every kind order against each other. A committed turn's events have
        contiguous sequences and are accepted with the commit; a rolled-back turn leaves one
        turn event with result "failed" and its code. An event is delivered only after the
        state it describes is published. Lifecycle events are not turns and never advance an
        actor revision. When sequence headroom runs out, admission refuses before any commit.
        Event kinds cover machines (turn, lifecycle, admission, send, schedule, context, issue,
        diagnostic), operations (resource, transaction, stream), entity domains (delta, field,
        repair, skip, lookup, prediction, queue, uncertain, answer, invalidate, tombstone,
        selfCheck, audit), persistence (capture, write, flush, restored, persistence-lock),
        lazy entries (entry-adopted, entry-adoption-failed) and server rendering (dehydrate,
        hydrate). A second tab emits persistence-lock with SecondTabNoPersistence and no
        capture or restore; no multi-tab coordination events exist in v1.

        A Story run produces a Trace (@plan.Testing.O4). trace.export(trace) returns canonical
        bytes and trace.import(bytes) validates them and returns a Trace; both are synchronous
        and need no App. A Trace holds kind and version, storyId, appId, appFingerprint,
        persistenceVersion, seed, a truncatedBeforeSequence marker, redacted events, uniquely
        named checkpoints, outcome ("completed" or "failed"), end, failure and cleanup status.
        It keeps at most 4,096 recent events, dropping the oldest until it fits the codec
        bounds and advancing the marker; checkpoints are never dropped, and if they cannot fit
        the export fails with CodecBoundExceeded.

        Import validates in order and rejects with one FlowError: the codec; kind, version and
        a closed schema (unknown or omitted members reject with InvalidTraceRecord); strictly
        increasing sequences consistent with the marker; lane and field correlations (Ready
        carries data, unknown carries reconcileRequired and an attempt id); lifecycle only
        active, then disposing, then disposed per incarnation (InvalidLifecycleTransition);
        and outcome consistency, so failed cleanup never yields completed
        (InvalidStoryEvidence). A Trace never contains callbacks, Effects, Layers, live refs,
        raw Cause, stacks or fiber ids, and is never a restore, hydrate or Story input.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/tools/inspection.ts (loadTrace).
    O5  Redaction and digests
        In-process reads, checkpoints, snapshots and inspect(runtime) carry raw values.
        Redaction applies where data leaves the process: sink events, trace artifacts and CLI
        output. Server-rendering payloads omit sensitive data entirely
        (@plan.ServerRendering.O3).

        A value under a Schema annotated sensitive: true, or a Schema.Redacted node, at any
        depth of an event, input, memory, context, key, result, error, stream value or entity
        field, leaves the process as "hmac:" plus 64 hex characters of HMAC-SHA-256 over its
        canonical bytes. Equal values under one key give equal digests, but a dictionary cannot
        recover them without the key. The key is 32 random bytes per Runtime, never exported
        or persisted. In Stories it is a private per-run key, reused when the same run repeats
        so repeated traces are identical, and not derivable from the public seed. A value that
        cannot be encoded as JSON is carried as { unencodable: <Schema identifier> }, never
        its contents. In artifacts, entity and resource data carry field names and digests,
        not values. Identities, versions, kinds, sequences, state paths, event tags and codes
        are never redacted. Digests are computed outside the turn.
    O6  Renderers
        render(model, "text" | "json" | "mermaid") is synchronous and deterministic. Text
        prints each machine's states, transitions, guards with deny codes, outcome edges,
        schedules and sends, and each domain as declaration sentences, with lazy entries
        marked. json is the canonical Model. mermaid draws a state diagram per machine and a
        flowchart of sends and context reads.

        Trace projections (printed by @plan.FlowCommandLine.O4) read one or two Traces and run
        no application code. summary gives counts and a per-actor timeline always stating
        "complete" or "truncated before N" and the outcome. Sections list normalized events by
        kind (transitions, sends, schedules, lifecycle, resources and why each refetched,
        transactions, repairs, restore, hydration, adoption, diagnostics). Filters are data:
        actor, attempt id, read, entity key with sequence, issues only. An unknown selector
        fails with InvalidArtifactOperand (reason UnknownSelector); missing evidence names
        EvidenceUnavailable. Under truncation, absence is reported as "insufficient evidence",
        never "none".

        Comparing two Traces has four results: equal (both complete, sections match),
        different (a retained fact differs, even if truncated), incomplete (retained facts
        match and an input is truncated), and the failure ArtifactIdentityMismatch when appId,
        appFingerprint, persistenceVersion or format version differ. coverage lists the
        declared transitions, deny codes, outcome edges, schedules and sends that no given
        Trace exercised.
decisions
    D1  The machine file is the spec
        The Model is the only structural representation; graph, diff, explain, check, render
        and the fingerprint all read it. No generated spec or saved structure file exists.
    D2  Deny codes are read from types, never by running guards
        The compiler reads each guard's literal return type. If complete facts are unavailable,
        inspect(app) rejects with TypeFactsUnavailable instead of returning an empty set as
        exhaustive. The compiler stays out of application bundles.
    D3  The fingerprint covers the whole Model
        appFingerprint is SHA-256 of the canonical Model excluding itself and run data, so any
        change to lazy content or deny codes changes it. There is no public fingerprint API.
    D4  Revisions are per incarnation and survive restore
        A new incarnation starts at 0; a restored or hydrated actor resumes its captured
        revision, so evidence compares across a reload.
constraints
    C1  Observation is never authority
        No mutable trace log, implicit history, fabricated trace, sink-owned actor work,
        snapshot replay or trace-driven restore exists; records become durable only through an
        explicit trace export. Artifact schemas are closed, and a format change bumps the
        version with no legacy importer.
    C2  Inspection stays out of application bundles
        flow-state, flow-state/react and flow-state/persistence never import
        flow-state/inspect, the compiler or a renderer.
verification
    V1  Model: a golden Model of the reference app; a spy shows no callback, adapter or Layer
        ran; a lazy variant equals the eager one except for loading; equal bytes on Node and a
        browser.
    V2  inspect(runtime): during a turn it returns the previous cut, never mixes revisions,
        starts no work, touches no storage and leaves the sequence unchanged.
    V3  Codec: each bound at its value and one past it; malformed UTF-8, BOM, lone surrogates,
        duplicate keys, -0 and non-canonical spellings fail with the right code and path;
        caller mutation of bytes changes nothing.
    V4  Sinks: a stalled sink never delays turns; a failing sink detaches alone; capacity 0,
        1 and default; attach before ready and late attach; exact markers; drain and dispose
        accepted-prefix behaviour.
    V5  Events and traces: one Story per event kind; contiguous turn sequences; a rolled-back
        turn leaves one failed event; export then import is byte-identical; every invalid
        import vector is rejected with its code; no Cause or stack appears in any artifact.
    V6  Redaction: sensitive values are digests in sink events and artifacts and raw in
        checkpoints; no sensitive value is found in any artifact; digests are equal under one
        key and not recoverable by a dictionary without it.
    V7  Renderers and projections: golden text, json and mermaid; every section and filter;
        the four comparison results; "insufficient evidence" under truncation; coverage.
questions
    Q1  Should a production Runtime be able to export a trace artifact?
        Recommended for version 1: no; hosts keep redacted sink events in their own logs.
    Q2  How does a host observe the private Runtime inside dehydrate?
        Recommended: dehydrate takes an optional inspector sink option attached before
        ready(); ServerRendering owns the option.
    Q3  Deferred to build time
        Exact event field lists; counters per turn; the 256-record delivery window;
        Model field layout; mermaid id derivation; domain-literal hashing; sequence headroom
        reservation; fingerprint ordering rules; supplying type facts outside source tooling.
